Title: "Limits and Continuity"
Id: "71b151a8-9a18-48b9-a4c4-3007044147d3"


Text: "Limits and continuity capture the rigorous mathematical essence of
       continuous change, geometric proximity, and local approximation.

       Formulated in the mid-nineteenth century by Karl Weierstrass, the
       $\epsilon$-$\delta$ definition dispenses with nebulous infinitesimals in
       favor of precise universal and existential quantifiers: a function $f$
       approaches $L$ at $x_0$ if for every tolerance $\epsilon > 0$ demanded in
       the output, there exists a response radius $\delta > 0$ in the input
       ensuring $|f(x) - L| < \epsilon$.

       Continuity at a point means that $f(x) \to f(x_0)$ as $x \to x_0$.
       Globally, continuous functions preserve the fundamental topological
       invariants of subsets: the continuous image of a connected set is
       connected (yielding the Intermediate Value Theorem), and the continuous
       image of a compact set is compact (yielding the Extreme Value Theorem and
       uniform continuity)."
Id: "800b7c32-41b4-4c27-a8d6-bb213da36a62"


SectionTitle: "Function Limits"
Id: "e33914da-aa4d-4409-a36b-9308d644ccc6"


[\real.function.limit:at{x0}:of{f}]
Defines: L is \real
using: U \:subset:/ \reals
when:
. x0 is \real
. f is \function:on{U}:to{\reals}
expresses:
. forAll: epsilon is \real
  where: epsilon \:real.greater?:/ \real.zero
  then:
  . exists: delta is \real
    suchThat:
    . delta \:real.greater?:/ \real.zero
    . forAll: x is \real
      where:
      . x "in" U
      . x != x0
      . \real.abs(x \.real.-./ x0) \:real.less?:/ delta
      then: \real.abs(f(x) \.real.-./ L) \:real.less?:/ epsilon
Documented:
. called: "limit of $f?$ at $x0?$"
. written: "\lim_{x \to x0?} f?(x)"
. description: "The limit of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $x_0$ equals $L$."
Id: "3af5db3a-ae38-4af7-876b-a775e8f83089"


SectionTitle: "Continuity"
Id: "550e6c6b-b2d6-4a7b-941c-de10e61ec6a1"


[\(continuous)::real.function:at{x0}]
Refines: f
using: U \:subset:/ \reals
when: x0 "in" U
specifies: f is \function:on{U}:to{\reals}
satisfies:
. forAll: epsilon is \real
  where: epsilon \:real.greater?:/ \real.zero
  then:
  . exists: delta is \real
    suchThat:
    . delta \:real.greater?:/ \real.zero
    . forAll: x is \real
      where:
      . x "in" U
      . \real.abs(x \.real.-./ x0) \:real.less?:/ delta
      then: \real.abs(f(x) \.real.-./ f(x0)) \:real.less?:/ epsilon
Documented:
. adjective: "continuous at $x0?$"
. description: "A function is continuous at a point $x_0$ if small changes in
                input yield arbitrarily small changes in output."
Id: "0596242b-c84c-4989-a566-a5e5d1a2c884"


[\(continuous)::real.function:on{U}]
Refines: f
when: U \:subset:/ \reals
specifies: f is \function:on{U}:to{\reals}
satisfies:
. forAll: x0 "in" U
  then: f is? \(continuous)::real.function:at{x0}
Documented:
. adjective: "continuous"
. description: "A function is continuous on a domain $U$ if it is continuous at
                every point of $U$."
Id: "5b60dfb1-186f-4583-9021-23830d2286df"


[\(uniformly.continuous)::real.function:on{U}]
Refines: f
when: U \:subset:/ \reals
specifies: f is \function:on{U}:to{\reals}
satisfies:
. forAll: epsilon is \real
  where: epsilon \:real.greater?:/ \real.zero
  then:
  . exists: delta is \real
    suchThat:
    . delta \:real.greater?:/ \real.zero
    . forAll: x, y is \real
      where:
      . x "in" U
      . y "in" U
      . \real.abs(x \.real.-./ y) \:real.less?:/ delta
      then: \real.abs(f(x) \.real.-./ f(y)) \:real.less?:/ epsilon
Documented:
. adjective: "uniformly continuous"
. description: "A function is uniformly continuous if a single $\delta$ works
                across the entire domain."
Id: "f69d925b-5ae6-4969-9d22-4d4eeea90a08"


SectionTitle: "The Intermediate Value Theorem"
Id: "53d3e7df-fd04-4eb3-9f05-149c234f9485"


TextTheorem: "The Intermediate Value Theorem (Bolzano). Let
              $f : [a, b] \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function on a closed
              interval with $a < b$. If $y$ lies between $f(a)$ and $f(b)$ (that
              is, $\min\{f(a), f(b)\} \leq y \leq \max\{f(a), f(b)\}$), then
              there exists at least one point $c \in [a, b]$ such that:
              $$ f(c) = y. $$

              *Proof*: Assume without loss of generality that $f(a) < y < f(b)$.
              Define the auxiliary continuous function $g(x) = f(x) - y$, so
              that $g(a) < 0 < g(b)$. We seek $c \in (a, b)$ with $g(c) = 0$.

              We construct a sequence of nested intervals $I_k = [a_k, b_k]$ via
              bisection, starting with $I_0 = [a, b]$. Having constructed $I_k$
              with $g(a_k) \leq 0 \leq g(b_k)$:
              1. Compute the midpoint $m = (a_k + b_k)/2$.
              2. If $g(m) = 0$, we are done: set $c = m$.
              3. If $g(m) < 0$, set $I_{k+1} = [m, b_k]$ (so
                 $a_{k+1} = m, b_{k+1} = b_k$).
              4. If $g(m) > 0$, set $I_{k+1} = [a_k, m]$ (so
                 $a_{k+1} = a_k, b_{k+1} = m$).

              By the Nested Interval Theorem,
              $\bigcap_{k=0}^\infty [a_k, b_k] = \{c\}$ for a unique point
              $c \in [a, b]$, with $\lim a_k = c = \lim b_k$. By continuity of
              $g$, $g(c) = \lim g(a_k) \leq 0$ and $g(c) = \lim g(b_k) \geq 0$.
              Consequently, $g(c) = 0$, which yields $f(c) = y$."
Id: "f29c7f02-8e4f-46da-ac4e-3df62dca57f0"


Text: "Both the continuity of $f$ and the connectedness of the interval $[a, b]$
       are indispensable to the Intermediate Value Theorem.

       A function with a jump discontinuity can skip directly from negative to
       positive values without attaining zero. Similarly, if the domain is
       disconnected (such as $[0, 1] \cup [2, 3]$), a continuous function may
       take negative values on the first component and positive values on the
       second without ever crossing zero. Topologically, the theorem states that
       the continuous image of a connected space is connected, and the connected
       subsets of $\mathbb{R}$ are precisely intervals."
Id: "580e742d-65ac-4bc1-a578-a5ab217a1b12"


SectionTitle: "Compactness and Uniform Control"
Id: "1c1dc16f-d9f4-431c-84ab-2a013263c6a4"


TextTheorem: "The Extreme Value Theorem (Weierstrass). If $f : [a, b] \to \mathbb{R}$
              is continuous on a closed bounded interval, then $f$ is bounded and attains
              both an absolute maximum and an absolute minimum on $[a, b]$:
              $$ \exists \, x_{\min}, x_{\max} \in [a, b] \quad \text{such that} \quad f(x_{\min}) \leq f(x) \leq f(x_{\max}) \quad \forall x \in [a, b]. $$
              
              *Proof Sketch*: Let $M = \sup\{f(x) : x \in [a, b]\} \in \mathbb{R} \cup \{+\infty\}$.
              Choose a sequence $(x_n)$ in $[a, b]$ such that $f(x_n) \to M$.
              Since $[a, b]$ is bounded, by the Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem $(x_n)$ has a
              convergent subsequence $x_{n_k} \to x_{\max}$.
              Since $[a, b]$ is closed, $x_{\max} \in [a, b]$.
              By continuity of $f$, $f(x_{\max}) = \lim_{k \to \infty} f(x_{n_k}) = M$.
              Since $f(x_{\max}) \in \mathbb{R}$, $M$ is finite and attained.
              The minimum is attained symmetrically by considering $-f$."
Id: "1de7e719-9ad4-4acd-a197-fe41c36077ea"


TextTheorem: "The Heine-Cantor Theorem. Every continuous function
              $f : [a, b] \to \mathbb{R}$ on a closed bounded interval is
              *uniformly continuous*.

              *Significance*: Pointwise continuity allows $\delta$ to shrink
              toward zero as $x$ approaches steep regions of the graph. On a
              compact interval $[a, b]$, no such unbounded steepening can occur:
              for any given $\epsilon > 0$, a single global $\delta > 0$
              suffices for all points simultaneously. This uniform control is
              the key technical requirement for proving the integrability of
              continuous functions."
Id: "bbd033d2-9fa7-47d7-9354-35da690e0f8e"


TextTheorem: "The Sequential Criterion for Limits and Continuity. Let
              $f : U \to \mathbb{R}$ and let $x_0$ be an accumulation point of
              $U$. Then $\lim_{x \to x_0} f(x) = L$ if and only if for every
              sequence $(x_n)$ in $U \setminus \{x_0\}$ converging to $x_0$, the
              sequence of outputs satisfies $f(x_n) \to L$.

              Consequently, $f$ is continuous at $x_0$ if and only if
              $f(\lim x_n) = \lim f(x_n)$ for every sequence converging to
              $x_0$. This criterion provides the operational bridge converting
              continuous analytical assertions into discrete sequence problems."
Id: "66ebdb25-bc98-4ea5-be68-0e57e72aa22e"
